owns are whether the phase transition will be sharp or gradual, how tight the convergence band stays across seeds, and what CodeLlama's tail-1024 v3 results will show.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the scenarios. CodeLlama's inconsistency is striking—98.24% on tail-1024 v3 but collapsing to 41.8% on tail-2048 v3—which muddies the narrative. The format marker contributes meaningfully but not overwhelmingly, so reviewers might argue we're partly capturing a formatting shortcut rather than true exception semantics. The real question is whether the pending experiments will tighten the story or expose more messiness. Best case is everything aligns cleanly with a sharp phase transition and tight seed curves. Median case involves softer claims and caveats as patterns get messier. Worst case is the phase transition shows no clear signal at all.\n\nI need to nail down the key forecasting metrics: how sharp is the tail-384 transition across the four models, how much variance do the cross-seed budget curves show, and what will reviewers actually accept given the single-task scope. Looking at the Qwen-7B numbers more carefully, there's a clear jump from tail-256 (77.48%) through tail-512 (93.6%) to tail-1024 (96.47%), then a smaller gain to tail-2048 (97.3%), which suggests the phase transition might already be visible in the data I have.\n\nThe critical question is whether tail-384 lands near tail-256 (sharp transition between 384-512) or interpolates smoothly toward tail-512 (gradual transition). A sharp transition would be the stronger narrative for the paper, but I need to see what the actual results show across all four models before committing to that framing. ation\n- Already transitioned: tail-384 ≈ 90%+, meaning transition is between 256-384\n\nFor the paper, either \"sharp\" or \"already transitioned\" is interesting — both show a phase transition, just at different points. \"Gradual\" is the worst because it undermines the \"phase transition\" framing entirely.\n\nLet me reconsider what constitutes best/median/worst holistically:\n\n**Best:** \n- tail-384 shows sharp transition (close to tail-256 or close to tail-512, not in between — either confirms transition between 384-512 or between 256-384)\n- Cross-seed budget curves tight\n- All 3 core claims hold without adjustment\n- Paper narrative: fault detection framing + phase transition + 4-model evidence = strong EMNLP Main submission\n\n**Median:**\n- tail-384 shows gradual transition (no clear phase transition, more like smooth curve)\n- Some seed variance in budget curves\n- Claims 1 and 3 hold, Claim 2 needs softening (information density story becomes \"diminishing returns\" rather than \"phase transition\")\n- Paper narrative needs adjustment: drop \"phase transition\" language, focus on \"tail-end diagnostic signal concentration\"\n\n**Worst:**\n- tail-384 data is noisy/inconsistent across models\n- Budget curves show high cross-seed variance\n- Phase transition claim doesn't hold\n- But: core exception_only dominance still holds (this is already well-established with 99.83%)\n- Reframe: pure empirical study of trace component importance, drop compression/phase transition claims, focus on diagnostic signal extraction as main contribution\n\nNow I'm looking at the actual numbers for each model to formalize this. Starting with Qwen-7B's tail budget curve, I can see tail-256 is around 78.9%.\n\nI'm noticing there are multiple naming conventions in the data — some entries use \"tail_256\" while others use \"tail256\", and I need to distinguish between tail truncation (last N tokens) versus head truncation (first N tokens). The tail measurements show values like 77.48% at tail-256, 80.42% at tail-512, and 77.23% at tail-1024, which suggests the tail-end tokens contain concentrated diagnostic signal rather than following a simple monotonic pattern.\n\nLooking at the tail truncation results across models, there's a sharp phase transition happening between 256 and 512 tokens — performance jumps from roughly 77% to 94-96% — and this pattern holds consistently across DeepSeek, Qwen-1.5B, and CodeLlama-13B, suggesting this is a fundamental property of how these models encode information about trace validity.\n\nNow I need to structure the YAML output carefully, defining what each tier means in the context of this specific research, laying out the key assumptions I'm testing (that exception signals are the primary mechanism, that a phase transition exists, and whether it's sharp or gradual), and establishing metric ranges that stay internally consistent with what I'm observing.\n\nThe nomarker ablation is already done—it's not hypothetical. The data shows format markers contribute roughly 2pp to performance, which is meaningful but not catastrophic. That's established ground I can work from when anticipating reviewer concerns.\n\nNow I need to think through what actually matters for the three tiers in the context of this specific paper and EMNLP submission timeline. The real differentiators are the tail-384 results across models, whether the current evidence package is convincing enough for Main track acceptance, and how sharp the phase transition looks. Those are the unknowns that will actually move the needle on reviewer reception.\n\nSo the tiers should reflect outcomes: Best case is when tail-384 shows a clean phase transition across models and the budget curve matrix tells a consistent story, letting all three claims stand at full strength. Median is messier — some models show sharp transitions, others gradual — which means I'd need to add caveats to the phase transition claim and probably do additional analysis to shore things up. Worst case is noisy tail-384 results that don't support the narrative cleanly.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific performance targets. For the best scenario, I'm expecting all four models to stay under 82% on tail-384, which would confirm a sharp transition between the 384 and 512 token ranges. For the median case, I'd see a split where some models show that sharp drop-off while others degrade more gradually.\n\nLooking at the cross-seed stability metrics at tail-512, I need to check whether the budget curves are actually reliable—if standard deviation exceeds 3 percentage points, that particular data point becomes questionable. Let me also pull in the cross-seed results from plan_017 to get a complete picture.\n\nActually, I'm getting too granular here. The real priority is identifying which scenarios are most informative and then documenting the findings in YAML format.\n\nI'm noticing something critical with CodeLlama's tail-2048 performance—it completely breaks down with accuracy around 30%, but switches to reasonable performance at tail-1024 where it hits 98% accuracy. This catastrophic failure at longer sequences is a key finding that needs to be reflected in how I tier the results.\n\nDeepSeek shows a similar but milder pattern where tail-2048 actually performs slightly worse than tail-1024, which challenges the straightforward \"more context is better\" story. These edge cases need to be woven into the paper's narrative regardless of how I structure the tiers.\n\nI'm focusing the tier structure on three things: whether tail-384 shows a clean phase transition, whether the budget curves across all four models tell a unified story or reveal model-specific behaviors, and what remaining P1 experiments could resolve the gaps. Now I'm compiling the budget curve data across models to see if there's a coherent pattern. Looking at the performance metrics across these models, I'm noticing CodeLlama's dramatic collapse at 2048 tokens is a significant issue—it could stem from how it tokenizes traces, how the loss gets diluted across longer sequences, or underlying training instability. This complicates the budget curve narrative, though it might be explainable as a training methodology artifact rather than a fundamental flaw. Now I'm checking what happens when we strip away the format markers, starting with Qwen-7B's results across different seeds.\n\nThe nomarker performance shows a consistent ~2pp gap across model scales, which actually strengthens the argument that format markers contribute minimally to the overall performance—the models are still achieving strong results through genuine semantic understanding of the exception data. This pattern holds for both Qwen-1.5B and CodeLlama, suggesting the format markers are a helpful shortcut but not the primary mechanism driving accuracy. I'm ready to synthesize these findings into the YAML structure.\n\nNow I'm looking at the zero and few-shot baselines to contextualize the SFT results. The gap between zero/few-shot and SFT is substantial—around 9-10pp for strong models like Qwen-7B, and dramatically larger for weaker models like CodeLlama at 49pp. This actually makes a compelling case for SFT over pure prompting approaches. The plan_020 experiment with GPT-4o and Claude few-shot is still pending, which would give me a frontier model comparison point, though that's marked as lower priority.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what the YAML needs to capture for the EMNLP decision. The best-case scenario is when all the P1 experiments confirm the core narrative—clean budget curves, sharp phase transitions, and the framing holds. That's a strong main conference submission. If results are messier, I might need to adjust claims but the fundamental story about exception dominance survives. Worst case, the P1 results contradict key parts of the narrative and the phase transition doesn't generalize, which would require reframing the paper. I should forecast the tail-384 accuracy across the four models and cross-seed variance at tail-512 to get a sense of result stability.\n\nThe phase transition sharpness is the key metric—I'm defining it as the ratio of (tail-384 minus tail-256) divided by (tail-512 minus tail-256). A ratio near zero means the transition happens sharply between 384 and 512 tokens, while a ratio near one means most of the improvement already happened by 384. For Qwen-7B specifically, if tail-256 is 77.5% and tail-512 is 93.6%, then in the best case tail-384 lands around 78-80%, giving a ratio of 0.03-0.15 and confirming that sharp transition. In the median scenario, tail-384 would be 84-88% with a more gradual ratio of 0.40-0.65. In the worst case, tail-384 hits 89-92%, suggesting the transition mostly completed before 384 tokens.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML structure. The core framing for each tier comes down to how well this paper would land against the EMNLP Main bar. In the best case, I get a sharp phase transition plus a clean budget matrix, which means I can claim three distinct contributions: exception dominance, the information density decomposition with phase transition as a novel finding, and the two-tier hierarchy—that's a strong Main paper. In the median case, the phase transition becomes less crisp or inconsistent across models, so I lose it as a standalone contribution and the budget curve becomes supporting evidence rather than its own finding, leaving me with 2.5 contributions where exception dominance is the strong anchor.\n\nIn the worst case, the budget matrix breaks down across models and the phase transition doesn't hold, so I need to pivot entirely toward the methodology itself—framing this as the first systematic component analysis for verdict tasks rather than claiming specific design principles. The key metrics I'll track are tail-384 accuracy per model, the phase transition ratio, whether all four models show the same qualitative pattern in the budget curve, cross-seed variance if available, and how the overall narrative holds together.\n\nNow I'm structuring the evaluation tiers in YAML, defining what \"best\" looks like: sharp phase transitions, consistent budget patterns across all four models, and all three claims holding at full strength for a strong EMNLP Main submission. The core narrative holds: exception dominance is the primary finding with solid evidence across all four models and multiple seeds, while the phase transition in the budget curve emerges as a secondary but crisp diagnostic signal showing that prediction collapses below the final 512 tokens. This gives me three clean contributions to present—exception dominance, information density with the phase transition and gradient dilution decomposition, and a two-tier diagnostic hierarchy supported by calibration evidence. Though there's some model-dependent variation in how sharp the phase transition appears, with CodeLlama showing a smoother curve than the others, the overall framing remains intact for EMNLP Main without needing to adjust the core claims. an outlier\"\n      - \"cross-seed budget curve shows moderate variance (σ 1-3pp)\"\n      - \"reviewers push back on scope (single task) but accept depth-over-breadth framing\"\n    number_ranges:\n      tail384_qwen7b: [83.0, 88.0]\n      tail384_deepseek: [82.0, 88.0]\n      tail384_qwen15b: [80.0, 87.0]\n      tail384_codellama13b: [83.0, 90.0]\n      phase_transition_ratio_mean: [0.30, 0.65]\n      budget_curve_monotonic_models: [2, 3]\n      cross_seed_tail512_std_max: [1.0, 3.0]\n    narrative_direction: \"Drop 'phase transition' language. Reframe Claim 2 as 'diminishing returns with trace length — diagnostic signal concentrates near trace end, but informativeness degrades gradually rather than abruptly.' Budget curve becomes supporting illustration for information density claim rather than standalone contribution. Strengthen paper with: (a) cross-model calibration analysis (plan_019) to add independent evidence dimension, (b) error analysis (plan_018) to provide qualitative depth, (c) emphasize exception_only's scale invariance (1.5B = 7B = 13B) as strongest empirical finding. Two strong contributions: (1) exception dominance, (3) two-tier hierarchy. Claim 2 becomes 'information density matters more than gradient dilution' without the phase transition component. EMNLP Main competitive, Findings as realistic backup.\"\n  \n  worst:\n    meaning: \"Budget curve matrix inconsistent across models: no universal trace length pattern, model-specific behaviors dominate → drop budget curve as contribution, reframe as pure trace component ablation study\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"tail-384 results vary widely across models (some sharp, some gradual, some non-monotonic)\"\n      - \"cross-seed budget curve shows high variance (σ > 3pp) undermining specific point estimates\"\n      - \"CodeLlama anomalies extend to other length points, not just 2048\"\n      - \"reviewers view 4-model evidence as confirming model-dependence rather than universality\"\n      - \"BUT: exception_only 99.83% across 4 models/scales is unassailable — this holds even in worst case\"\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance ranges across different model configurations and how they cluster. The tail-384 results show tighter consistency in the best scenario compared to the worst, with standard deviations that either support or undermine the phase transition narrative depending on how the data breaks. The key tension is whether the budget curve pattern holds universally enough to be a standalone contribution, or whether I should anchor the paper entirely on the exception dominance finding and the two-tier hierarchy, using the trace length analysis as supporting evidence rather than a primary claim.\n\nI'm reconsidering the framing: exception events emerge as the only reliable signal that generalizes across model families and scales, which is actually stronger than the budget curve story since it doesn't depend on specific architectural choices. The format marker removal and the zero-shot to SFT progression both validate that we're capturing genuine semantic learning rather than noise. The irreducible error ceiling at 99.83% suggests we've hit a representation limit, not a measurement artifact. So the reframe drops the budget curve as a standalone contribution and instead positions exception events as the minimal sufficient signal for fault detection, validated across multiple model families and scales. This keeps the ambition high—it's a systematic trace component study—but with a clearer, more defensible core claim.\n\nNow I'm checking the metric consistency across the tail384_qwen7b results. The user wants best ≥ median ≥ worst ordering, but I'm noticing that lower accuracy at tail-384 actually supports the sharp phase transition story better, which inverts the typical \"higher is better\" assumption. I need to clarify whether the metric ordering should follow the paper's narrative or stick to conventional metric interpretation.\n\nLet me reframe this using phase_transition_sharpness and budget_curve_consistency instead, where higher values consistently indicate better outcomes and maintain the monotonic ordering the user requested. I see the constraint now—the user wants consistent ordering across all metrics, so I need to flip metrics where lower is actually better into their inverse forms. That way, best ≥ median ≥ worst holds uniformly. So I'll use phase_transition_sharpness as 1 minus the ratio, budget_curve_r2 for consistency, and claims_surviving_full_strength, ensuring all metrics follow the same hierarchical pattern without contradictions. what the key evaluation criteria are. The user wants to assess whether the current plan is strong enough for EMNLP main track, focusing on evidence strength for the three core claims, the marginal contribution of the phase transition experiments, whether the budget curve analysis across models tells a compelling story, and some additional criteria that got cut off. So I need to structure the metrics around these specific evaluation dimensions rather than generic performance indicators. 我在权衡这些实验对论文评审的影响。如果P1实验显示清晰的相位转变，加上预算曲线矩阵和异常主导性，就能构成三个独立的强贡献，label_only_loss作为支撑证据足够，预期评分3.5-4.0。如果转变是渐进的，预算曲线退为辅助证据，需要更强调异常的尺度不变性和跨模型鲁棒性，label_only_loss变得更关键，大约2.5个贡献，评分3.0-3.5。最坏情况下P